<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polo Forest</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polo Forest is a forested area near Vijaynagar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabarkantha-district">Sabarkantha district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, at the foot of the Aravalli hills on the Harnav river. It contains ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Jain temples dating from about the 10th to the 15th centuries and remains of an old fortified town, and it is now a protected forest visited for its monuments and wildlife.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polo-forest.jpg" alt="Polo Forest" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Naresh Darji · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polo_Forest_Area.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Polo Forest, also called Vijaynagar Forest, is a dry mixed deciduous forest near Abhapur village in Vijaynagar Taluka, spread over about 400 square kilometres on the banks of the perennial Harnav River. It marks the northern limit of teak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, since teak does not grow north of the valley. More than 450 species of medicinal plants, around 275 species of birds and 30 of mammals have been recorded there, among them sloth bears, leopards, hyenas, four-horned antelopes and flying squirrels, and migratory birds arrive in winter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parihar">Parihar</a> kings of Idar founded a town beside the Harnav, probably in the 10th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathores of Marwar</a> conquered it in the 15th century, and it came under Idar State. The town lay between two high hills, Kalaliyo and Mamrehchi, which block the sun for most of the day, and this probably led to its abandonment. The name Polo comes from pol, a gate in Marwari and Gujarati.</p><p>Most of the surviving temples date from the 15th century. The three-storeyed Sharaneshwar temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> at Abhapur stands within a fortified wall with gates on the east and west, its walls carved with Yama, Bhairava, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>, Parvati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a>, though its shikhara has fallen. A hero stone in its compound, dated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikram-samvat">Vikram Samvat</a> 1554, names Rao Bhana as ruler of Idar. The Jain temples known as Lakhena na Dera include a large sandstone temple with carved ceilings, pierced stone screens and an image of the Tirthankara Parshwanatha above the sanctum door; it was once surrounded by 52 small shrines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The temples, among them the Sharaneshwar temple, the Surya Mandir and Sadevant Savlinga na Dera, are restored and managed by the state archaeology <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. The Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> holds the Polo Festival there every year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polo Forest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The area is known for its ruined temples at <b>Polo</b>, deep in the forest near the old capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/vijaynagar-state">Vijaynagar State</a></li></ul></section>
